# PART VII. ANIMATION SYSTEM

## 7. ANIMATION PHILOSOPHY

Motion should communicate depth, hierarchy, and feedback.

It must never compromise usability.

Use three animation categories:

1. Entrance animations.
2. Interactive feedback.
3. Decorative background motion.

Give each category its own timing rules.

## 7.1 Entrance animations

Use a carefully staged sequence on initial page load.

Suggested order:

1. Background texture is immediately available.
2. Navigation appears with a subtle fade.
3. Hero eyebrow fades into view.
4. Headline enters with a gentle upward movement.
5. Supporting paragraph appears.
6. Primary and secondary buttons appear with a restrained stagger.
7. Glass composition settles into place.
8. Decorative background geometry begins its slow motion.

Keep the animation sequence brief enough that users can access the interface promptly.

Use opacity and small transforms rather than large movements.

Avoid extreme blur transitions on text.

Text must become readable quickly.

Do not make the entire application wait for decorative animations to finish.

## 7.2 Suggested timing

Use the following as initial design guidance:

| Animation | Suggested duration |
|---|---:|
| Small button feedback | 140-220 ms |
| Hover surface transition | 180-260 ms |
| Modal entrance | 220-320 ms |
| Card entrance | 300-500 ms |
| Hero text entrance | 450-750 ms |
| Background geometric movement | 20-45 seconds per cycle |

These values are starting points.

Refine them to achieve a composed and responsive experience.

The background movement should be significantly slower than interaction feedback.

## 7.3 Easing

Use smooth, restrained easing.

Avoid bouncy, elastic, exaggerated, or cartoon-like movement.

Use small translate distances.

Avoid unnecessary scaling that makes controls feel unstable.

## 7.4 Reduced motion

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

When reduced motion is requested:

- Disable continuous decorative rotation.
- Reduce entrance movements.
- Remove unnecessary parallax.
- Avoid large transforms.
- Preserve all functionality.
- Keep interaction feedback clear.

The interface must remain fully usable without animation.

